{"_id":"encSysJournal01","name":"Encounter System — How to Use & Extend","pages":[{"_id":"encSysPage01","name":"How to Use the Encounter System","type":"text","title":{"show":true,"level":1},"text":{"format":1,"markdown":"","content":"<article style=\"font-family: Georgia, serif; color: #29231e; line-height: 1.42; max-width: 980px; margin: 0 auto;\"><div style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #3b2520, #76533d); color: #f8edcf; padding: 22px 26px; border: 3px solid #b89054; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 3px 10px rgba(0,0,0,.25);\"><div style=\"font-size: 12px; letter-spacing: 2px; text-transform: uppercase; opacity: .85;\">Foundry VTT Campaign Reference</div><h1 style=\"margin: 5px 0; color: #fff3d2; font-size: 30px;\">Encounter System</h1><div style=\"font-style: italic; font-size: 16px;\">Generate journeys, weather and encounters from editable RollTables</div></div><div style=\"background: #f3ead8; border: 1px solid #c9b58e; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0; border-radius: 6px;\"><h2 style=\"margin-top: 0; color: #5a3427;\">What this module does</h2><p>The Encounter System is a system-neutral travel and settlement encounter generator. It creates a multi-day journey card in chat, rolls the weather for each day, uses the standard three-part Road/River schedule or the six-part Wilderness schedule, and then resolves each encounter with distance, awareness and reaction.</p><p>It is table-driven: the tables in the world are the content. You can edit, replace or expand them without changing the basic journey workflow, provided the required names and result format are preserved.</p></div><h2 style=\"color: #5a3427; border-bottom: 2px solid #b89054; padding-bottom: 4px;\">1. Generate a journey</h2><ol><li>Open the <strong>Encounter System</strong> macro or toolbar button.</li><li>Choose the <strong>Route</strong>: Road, River or Wilderness. If Road or River is selected, optionally choose the terrain through which it passes. If Wilderness is selected, choose Forest, Marsh, Hills, Mountains or Jungle.</li><li>Choose the <strong>Season</strong>: Spring, Summer, Autumn or Winter.</li><li>Enter the number of <strong>Days</strong> to generate, from 1 to 30.</li><li>A GM may leave <strong>Show results to players</strong> checked, or clear it to whisper the journey card to the GM group.</li><li>Click <strong>Generate</strong>. The result appears as one chat card containing every day and every time period.</li></ol><div style=\"background: #eee4cf; border-left: 5px solid #8a5a3b; padding: 11px 15px; margin: 14px 0;\"><strong>Keep the “No Encounter” lines.</strong> They show that the check was made and that the journey period was uneventful; they are not missing results.</div><h2 style=\"color: #5a3427; border-bottom: 2px solid #b89054; padding-bottom: 4px;\">2. What happens each day</h2><p>For every generated day the macro:</p><ol><li>Draws one result for each travel period from the matching seasonal environment table, such as <strong>Weather Table - Forest - Spring</strong>.</li><li>For Road or River, makes checks at <strong>Morning</strong>, <strong>Afternoon</strong> and <strong>Night</strong>. For Wilderness, makes checks at <strong>Morning</strong>, <strong>Late Morning</strong>, <strong>Late Afternoon</strong>, <strong>Evening Watch</strong>, <strong>Night Watch</strong> and <strong>Deepest Night</strong>.</li><li>On a route success, draws from the Road or River table. If a route background terrain was selected, there is a separate <strong>20% chance</strong> that the matching terrain Day/Night table is used instead. Weather uses the route terrain when one is selected; otherwise it uses the route or pure wilderness environment. The first period of each day rolls fresh weather; later periods normally continue the previous conditions, with a 25% chance to change. Wilderness always draws from its pure environment table.</li><li>Resolves the encounter using its Size, Motivation and Visibility tags.</li><li>Has a separate 5% chance of adding an <strong>Additional Encounter</strong> in the same period.</li></ol><table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 10px 0 16px; font-size: 14px;\"><thead><tr style=\"background: #5a3427; color: #fff3d2;\"><th style=\"padding: 8px; text-align: left;\">Route or wilderness</th><th style=\"padding: 8px; text-align: left;\">Day check</th><th style=\"padding: 8px; text-align: left;\">Night check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr style=\"background: #f5eee1;\"><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #d8c8aa;\">Road</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #d8c8aa;\">20%</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #d8c8aa;\">25%</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #d8c8aa;\">River</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #d8c8aa;\">25%</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #d8c8aa;\">30%</td></tr><tr style=\"background: #f5eee1;\"><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #d8c8aa;\">Forest</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #d8c8aa;\">25%</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #d8c8aa;\">30%</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #d8c8aa;\">Marsh</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #d8c8aa;\">25%</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #d8c8aa;\">49%</td></tr><tr style=\"background: #f5eee1;\"><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #d8c8aa;\">Hills</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #d8c8aa;\">20%</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #d8c8aa;\">25%</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #d8c8aa;\">Mountains</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #d8c8aa;\">25%</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #d8c8aa;\">30%</td></tr><tr style=\"background: #f5eee1;\"><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Jungle</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">30%</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">40%</td></tr></tbody></table><p style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #6e5a49;\"><em>The check uses 1d100: an equal or lower result is an encounter.</em></p><h2 style=\"color: #5a3427; border-bottom: 2px solid #b89054; padding-bottom: 4px;\">3. Encounter resolution</h2><p>An encounter result must include three machine-readable tags in its name. The required format is:</p><div style=\"background: #2d2723; color: #f8edcf; padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 5px; font-family: monospace; font-size: 14px;\"><strong>Encounter name (Size, Motivation, Visibility)</strong><br><br>Examples:<br>Wolves (Small, Hostile, Obvious)<br>Travellers (Medium, Indifferent, Normal)<br>A scout patrol (Large, Friendly, Stealthy)</div><p>Allowed values are:</p><ul><li><strong>Size:</strong> Small, Medium or Large.</li><li><strong>Motivation:</strong> Friendly, Indifferent or Hostile.</li><li><strong>Visibility:</strong> Stealthy, Normal or Obvious.</li></ul><p>The macro then:</p><ol><li>Draws the base result from <strong>Distance Encountered</strong>.</li><li>Adjusts distance for Size, Visibility and weather.</li><li>Rolls awareness internally with 1d100 and applies the same modifiers.</li><li>Uses the matching reaction table—<strong>Friendly Reaction</strong>, <strong>Indifferent Reaction</strong> or <strong>Hostile Reaction</strong>—unless the encounter is surprised or unaware and the party acts first.</li></ol><p><strong>Weather effects:</strong> each period evaluates weather separately, normally continuing the previous period’s conditions with a 25% chance of change. Rain, snow, sleet, hail, fog or mist moves that period’s encounter one distance step closer and applies Awareness +10. A downpour does the same with Awareness +20.</p><h2 style=\"color: #5a3427; border-bottom: 2px solid #b89054; padding-bottom: 4px;\">4. Required RollTables</h2><table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 10px 0 16px; font-size: 14px;\"><thead><tr style=\"background: #5a3427; color: #fff3d2;\"><th style=\"padding: 8px; text-align: left;\">Purpose</th><th style=\"padding: 8px; text-align: left;\">Exact table names</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr style=\"background: #f5eee1;\"><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #d8c8aa;\">Weather</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #d8c8aa;\">Weather Table - [Environment] - [Season], for example Weather Table - Forest - Spring; the legacy seasonal tables remain as fallback tables</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #d8c8aa;\">Environment encounters</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #d8c8aa;\">Road Day, Road Night; River Day, River Night; Forest Day, Forest Night; Marsh Day, Marsh Night; Hills Day, Hills Night; Mountains Day, Mountains Night; Jungle Day, Jungle Night</td></tr><tr style=\"background: #f5eee1;\"><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Distance</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Distance Encountered</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Reaction</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Friendly Reaction, Indifferent Reaction, Hostile Reaction</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Important:</strong> the macro searches World RollTables by exact name. If a table is sitting only in a compendium, import it into the world first. Spelling and capitalisation matter.</p><h2 style=\"color: #5a3427; border-bottom: 2px solid #b89054; padding-bottom: 4px;\">5. Editing or adding content</h2><p>To customise an existing environment, duplicate its Day and Night tables, keep the exact table names, and edit the result entries. Keep the three parenthetical tags on every encounter result. This is the safest way to add new creatures, travellers, hazards or settlement events.</p><p>The selector uses <strong>Road, River or Wilderness</strong> as the route choice. Road and River can each be given a background terrain. Wilderness uses the pure Forest, Marsh, Hills, Mountains or Jungle tables and the six wilderness periods. It does not discover a new environment automatically from a table name alone. To add something such as Meadow:</p><ol><li>Add <strong>Meadow</strong> to the environment list in the generator macro.</li><li>Create exact World RollTables named <strong>Meadow Day</strong> and <strong>Meadow Night</strong>.</li><li>Format every result as <strong>Name (Size, Motivation, Visibility)</strong>.</li><li>Test both a daylight and a night journey before using it in play.</li></ol><div style=\"background: #e8dfcf; border-left: 5px solid #b89054; padding: 11px 15px; margin: 14px 0;\"><strong>Practical rule:</strong> copy a working environment table first. The table title selects the environment and time; the parenthetical result tags drive distance, awareness and reaction.</div><h2 style=\"color: #5a3427; border-bottom: 2px solid #b89054; padding-bottom: 4px;\">6. Troubleshooting</h2><ul><li><strong>“Missing table”:</strong> check that the table is imported as a World RollTable and that its name exactly matches the required title.</li><li><strong>“Could not parse”:</strong> check the final parentheses, commas and spelling of Small/Medium/Large, Friendly/Indifferent/Hostile and Stealthy/Normal/Obvious.</li><li><strong>No reaction appears:</strong> this is intentional when the party has surprise or the encounter is unaware; the party acts first.</li><li><strong>Weather has no effect:</strong> the weather result must contain the words <em>rain</em>, <em>snow</em> or <em>downpour</em> for the automatic modifier to apply.</li><li><strong>New environment is ignored:</strong> add it to the macro’s environment list as well as creating its Day/Night tables.</li></ul><div style=\"background: #3b2520; color: #f8edcf; padding: 15px 18px; border-radius: 6px; margin-top: 16px;\"><strong>GM quick reference:</strong> choose Route, optional route terrain or Wilderness environment, Season and Days → generate → read Weather → make or accept the route or six Wilderness period results → resolve encounters through the tagged result, Distance Encountered and the appropriate Reaction table.</div><p style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #6e5a49; margin-top: 18px;\"><em>Designed for the Encounter System module and its editable Foundry World RollTables.</em></p></article>"},"video":{"volume":0.5,"autoplay":false,"loop":false},"image":{"caption":""},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":0,"ownership":{"default":2},"flags":{}}],"folder":null,"sort":0,"ownership":{"default":2},"flags":{}}
{"_id":"qR7mT2vK9pL4xN6C","name":"WFRP Road Travel & Mount Fatigue","pages":[{"_id":"rT4nY8kP2vM6sQ9C","name":"Road Travel & Mount Fatigue","type":"text","title":{"show":true,"level":1},"text":{"format":1,"markdown":"","content":"<article style=\"font-family: Georgia, serif; color: #25211d; line-height: 1.42; max-width: 980px; margin: 0 auto;\"><div style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #33251c, #6e4b2e); color: #f6ead0; padding: 22px 26px; border: 3px solid #b89054; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 3px 10px rgba(0,0,0,.25);\"><div style=\"font-size: 12px; letter-spacing: 2px; text-transform: uppercase; opacity: .85;\">WFRP Campaign Reference</div><h1 style=\"margin: 5px 0; color: #fff3d2; font-size: 30px;\">Road Travel &amp; Mount Fatigue</h1><div style=\"font-style: italic; font-size: 16px;\">Practical journey times, road delays, care and recovery</div></div><div style=\"background: #f4ead2; border: 1px solid #c7aa77; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0; border-radius: 6px;\"><h2 style=\"margin-top: 0; color: #5c351e;\">Using this page</h2><p>Choose the closest transport type, divide the route distance by its normal daily rate and round up. Then apply road, weather, toll, encounter and care delays.</p><p><strong>Formula:</strong> travel days = route miles ÷ daily rate, rounded up. These are campaign benchmarks, not racing speeds.</p></div><h2 style=\"color: #5c351e; border-bottom: 2px solid #b89054; padding-bottom: 4px;\">Daily travel benchmarks</h2><table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 10px 0 16px; font-size: 14px;\"><thead><tr style=\"background: #5c351e; color: #fff3d2;\"><th style=\"padding: 8px; text-align: left;\">Transport</th><th style=\"padding: 8px; text-align: left;\">Normal rate</th><th style=\"padding: 8px; text-align: left;\">Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr style=\"background: #f6efdf;\"><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbb58e;\"><strong>Fast Coach</strong></td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbb58e;\">50–60 mi</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbb58e;\">Fresh relay teams, maintained route and priority passage.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbb58e;\"><strong>Normal Coach</strong></td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbb58e;\">35–45 mi</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbb58e;\">Loading, passengers, repairs, traffic and ordinary toll stops.</td></tr><tr style=\"background: #f6efdf;\"><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbb58e;\"><strong>Courier Horse</strong></td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbb58e;\">55–65 mi</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbb58e;\">Assumes fresh relay mounts. One horse uses the riding-horse rule.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbb58e;\"><strong>Riding Horse</strong></td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbb58e;\">40–50 mi</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbb58e;\">Use 45 miles as the standard good-road rate.</td></tr><tr style=\"background: #f6efdf;\"><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbb58e;\"><strong>Light Cart</strong></td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbb58e;\">25–35 mi</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbb58e;\">One draught horse; hills, mud, loading and repairs slow it.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbb58e;\"><strong>Heavy Wagon</strong></td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbb58e;\">15–25 mi</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbb58e;\">Heavy loads and poor roads; very vulnerable to gradients and mud.</td></tr><tr style=\"background: #f6efdf;\"><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbb58e;\"><strong>Donkey or Mule</strong></td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbb58e;\">20–30 mi</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbb58e;\">Hardy and sure-footed; 15–25 miles with heavy loads or rough ground.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding: 7px;\"><strong>On Foot</strong></td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">20–30 mi</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Weather, packs and road quality have a major effect.</td></tr></tbody></table><div style=\"background: #eee2c5; border-left: 5px solid #8d5b32; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 16px 0;\"><strong>Altdorf–Middenheim:</strong> the 362-mile route takes an independent riding party approximately 9 days. The special Fast Coach timetable takes 6 days because it changes teams and receives priority passage. Travel stages are narrative milestones and do not replace calendar days.</div><h2 style=\"color: #5c351e; border-bottom: 2px solid #b89054; padding-bottom: 4px;\">Road, weather and tolls</h2><table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 10px 0 16px; font-size: 14px;\"><thead><tr style=\"background: #5c351e; color: #fff3d2;\"><th style=\"padding: 8px; text-align: left;\">Condition</th><th style=\"padding: 8px; text-align: left;\">Adjustment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr style=\"background: #f6efdf;\"><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Firm Imperial highway</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Use the normal rate.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Minor road, forest or hills</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Reduce daily miles by 10–25%.</td></tr><tr style=\"background: #f6efdf;\"><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Rain, mud or strong headwind</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Reduce by 25%, or add a day to a short journey.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Downpour, snow or difficult ground</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Reduce by 50% and consider an extra stable night.</td></tr><tr style=\"background: #f6efdf;\"><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Blizzard, flood or blocked road</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Stop, seek shelter or take a major detour.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Toll houses</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Expect them every 20–40 miles. Private travellers may need to stop, pay and answer questions; established coaches pass more quickly.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 style=\"color: #5c351e; border-bottom: 2px solid #b89054; padding-bottom: 4px;\">Mount fatigue: the two-day rule</h2><p><strong>Days 1–2:</strong> A mount may travel its full normal rate on both days if it receives food, water and a proper overnight rest. No mount Endurance test is required.</p><p><strong>Day 3:</strong> After two consecutive travel days, test the mount's <strong>Endurance (Toughness)</strong> at <strong>Difficult (–10)</strong> to travel beyond half its normal rate.</p><table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 10px 0 16px; font-size: 14px;\"><thead><tr style=\"background: #5c351e; color: #fff3d2;\"><th style=\"padding: 8px; text-align: left;\">Day 3 result</th><th style=\"padding: 8px; text-align: left;\">Maximum travel</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr style=\"background: #f6efdf;\"><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Failure</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">50% of the normal rate</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Success</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">75% of the normal rate</td></tr><tr style=\"background: #f6efdf;\"><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Success by 2+ SL with +20 or more care modifiers</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Full normal rate</td></tr></tbody></table><p>If final modifiers total less than +10, the mount cannot exceed half speed on Day 3 even if the test is passed.</p><h3 style=\"color: #5c351e;\">Day 3 care modifiers</h3><ul><li><strong>+10:</strong> full feed and clean water for both days.</li><li><strong>+10:</strong> good stable, dry bedding and grooming on the second night.</li><li><strong>+10:</strong> successful Animal Care test, including hoof and tack check.</li><li><strong>–10:</strong> poor camp, limited forage, heavy load, bad road, severe weather or inadequate water.</li></ul><p>Cap combined care modifiers at <strong>+30/–20</strong>. A well-supplied campsite may count as good care for donkeys and mules.</p><p><strong>Day 4+ without a full rest day:</strong> test again at <strong>Very Difficult (–20)</strong>. Success permits a maximum of half normal travel; failure causes collapse, lameness or a riding mishap. A full rest day resets the count to Day 1.</p><h2 style=\"color: #5c351e; border-bottom: 2px solid #b89054; padding-bottom: 4px;\">Care, camps and stops</h2><ul><li><strong>Before departure:</strong> water, forage, tack inspection and hoof check.</li><li><strong>Every 3–4 hours:</strong> dismount, offer water and allow a short rest. This is included in the daily rates.</li><li><strong>On arrival:</strong> remove tack, cool and groom the mount, inspect hooves and back, then provide water and forage.</li><li><strong>Stable:</strong> best recovery; provides shelter, hay, water, grooming, checks and tack repairs.</li><li><strong>Road camp:</strong> use the wilderness night table; secure the animals and provide water, forage and weather protection.</li><li><strong>Normal riding horse:</strong> two road nights are acceptable; plan a village, coaching inn or farm stable by the third night.</li><li><strong>Cart or wagon:</strong> draught animals need a yard or secure farm stop each night.</li></ul><h2 style=\"color: #5c351e; border-bottom: 2px solid #b89054; padding-bottom: 4px;\">Using the Encounter System</h2><ol><li>Calculate calendar days from the transport table.</li><li>Run the <strong>Morning, Afternoon and Night</strong> checks for each travel day.</li><li>Apply road, weather, toll, repair and care delays before deciding whether the planned stop is reached.</li><li>Use settlement results for stable nights and the wilderness night table for road camps.</li><li>A failed test does not automatically kill a mount; death is only possible if a collapsed animal is forced onwards and then fails a final Endurance test.</li></ol><p style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #6b5a45; margin-top: 18px;\"><em>Source basis: WFRP Core, Enemy in Shadows Companion and Power Behind the Throne. Mileage figures are practical campaign benchmarks built around the official Movement, travel, mount endurance, coaching-house and travel-stage rules.</em></p></article>"},"video":{"volume":0.5,"autoplay":false,"loop":false},"image":{"caption":""},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":0,"ownership":{"default":2},"flags":{}}],"folder":null,"sort":0,"ownership":{"default":2},"flags":{}}
{"_id":"vR6mT2pK9xL4qN7C","name":"WFRP River Travel & Settlement Stops","pages":[{"_id":"rV4nY8kP2mM6sQ9C","name":"River Travel & Settlement Stops","type":"text","title":{"show":true,"level":1},"text":{"format":1,"markdown":"","content":"<article style=\"font-family: Georgia, serif; color: #25211d; line-height: 1.42; max-width: 980px; margin: 0 auto;\"><div style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #243c46, #496b70); color: #f6ead0; padding: 22px 26px; border: 3px solid #b89054; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 3px 10px rgba(0,0,0,.25);\"><div style=\"font-size: 12px; letter-spacing: 2px; text-transform: uppercase; opacity: .85;\">WFRP Campaign Reference</div><h1 style=\"margin: 5px 0; color: #fff3d2; font-size: 30px;\">River Travel &amp; Settlement Stops</h1><div style=\"font-style: italic; font-size: 16px;\">Currents, locks, weather, hazards and boat maintenance</div></div><div style=\"background: #e8f0ed; border: 1px solid #9bb5b2; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0; border-radius: 6px;\"><h2 style=\"margin-top: 0; color: #24434a;\">Using this page</h2><p>Choose the vessel and direction, divide the route distance by its daily rate and round up. The boat travels its full normal distance each day. Delays or lost distance occur only because of weather, locks, hazards, repairs or a mishap.</p><p><strong>Encounter System:</strong> run the <strong>Morning, Afternoon and Night</strong> checks every travel day. Use the River Day table during daylight and the River Night table after dark.</p><p><strong>River encounter baseline:</strong> River Day 30%; River Night 40%.</p></div><h2 style=\"color: #24434a; border-bottom: 2px solid #8da7a5; padding-bottom: 4px;\">River travel benchmarks</h2><table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 10px 0 16px; font-size: 14px;\"><thead><tr style=\"background: #24434a; color: #fff3d2;\"><th style=\"padding: 8px; text-align: left;\">Vessel and direction</th><th style=\"padding: 8px; text-align: left;\">Normal miles/day</th><th style=\"padding: 8px; text-align: left;\">Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr style=\"background: #edf3f0;\"><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #b8ccca;\"><strong>Trading barge downstream</strong></td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #b8ccca;\">35–45</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #b8ccca;\">Use 40 as the normal benchmark.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #b8ccca;\"><strong>Trading barge upstream</strong></td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #b8ccca;\">25–35</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #b8ccca;\">Use 30; towing, rowing and tacking take time.</td></tr><tr style=\"background: #edf3f0;\"><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #b8ccca;\"><strong>Fast passenger or sailing vessel</strong></td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #b8ccca;\">38–45</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #b8ccca;\">Requires a capable crew and favourable conditions.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #b8ccca;\"><strong>Rowboat or small skiff</strong></td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #b8ccca;\">15–25</td><td style=\"padding: 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #b8ccca;\">Short journeys unless the crew can rotate.</td></tr><tr style=\"background: #edf3f0;\"><td style=\"padding: 7px;\"><strong>Disabled or towed boat</strong></td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Half normal rate</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Boat Handling Tests suffer –10.</td></tr></tbody></table><p style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #536b6b;\"><em>The Emperor Luitpold provides an upper benchmark of about 45 miles downstream and 38 upstream. Ordinary trading barges are slower because they carry cargo and have smaller crews.</em></p><h2 style=\"color: #24434a; border-bottom: 2px solid #8da7a5; padding-bottom: 4px;\">Daily boat maintenance</h2><p>At the end of each travel day, make one <strong>Boat Maintenance Test</strong>. Use <strong>Trade (Carpenter)</strong> for structural work, or <strong>Row/Sail</strong> for routine work when performed by a competent boatman. <strong>Lore (Riverways)</strong> grants +1 SL. This test represents checking the hull, bilge, rudder, ropes, sail, oars, cargo lashings and moorings.</p><table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 10px 0 16px; font-size: 14px;\"><thead><tr style=\"background: #24434a; color: #fff3d2;\"><th style=\"padding: 8px; text-align: left;\">Consecutive travel day</th><th style=\"padding: 8px; text-align: left;\">Maintenance modifier</th><th style=\"padding: 8px; text-align: left;\">Travel distance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr style=\"background: #edf3f0;\"><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Day 1</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">+0</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Full normal rate</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Day 2</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">–10</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Full normal rate</td></tr><tr style=\"background: #edf3f0;\"><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Day 3</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">–20</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Full normal rate</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Day 4+</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">–30 maximum</td><td style=\"padding: 7px;\">Full normal rate, but rising mishap risk</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Use a base difficulty appropriate to the boat and condition, normally <strong>Challenging (+0)</strong>. The consecutive-day modifier is applied to that test only; it does not reduce movement.</p><h3 style=\"color: #24434a;\">Failed maintenance tests</h3><p>A failed test causes a <strong>Boat Mishap</strong>. The mishap may involve a loose rope, fouled rudder, leaking hull, damaged oar, broken rigging, lost cargo, grounding, crew injury or an unexpected delay. A serious failure may prevent further travel until the boat is repaired.</p><p><strong>Important:</strong> the boat does not automatically lose miles because the maintenance test became harder. Only the actual mishap can cause delay, damage or lost progress.</p><h2 style=\"color: #24434a; border-bottom: 2px solid #8da7a5; padding-bottom: 4px;\">Why stop at a settlement every third night?</h2><p>Riverboats stop because the river’s infrastructure is built around regular lock-houses, toll points, quays and riverside settlements—not because the boat is incapable of travelling further.</p><ul><li><strong>Supplies:</strong> refill water casks and obtain food, lamp oil, firewood and other necessities.</li><li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> obtain pitch, rope, nails, timber, sailcloth and proper tools; inspect the boat out of the water where possible.</li><li><strong>Locks and tolls:</strong> river toll points commonly occur every 20–30 miles, often near a lock, bridge, quay or settlement.</li><li><strong>Cargo:</strong> pay tolls, check manifests, load or unload goods and secure the cargo properly.</li><li><strong>Security:</strong> use a licensed quay, lock-house or patrol base rather than an exposed bank vulnerable to wreckers, pirates, floods and thieves.</li><li><strong>Crew:</strong> allow boat-hands to sleep ashore, recover from rowing and rope work, and replace exhausted or injured crew.</li></ul><div style=\"background: #dce9e5; border-left: 5px solid #4b7778; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 16px 0;\"><strong>Campaign ruling:</strong> aim for a proper river stop on the third night. It may be a town, village quay, riverside inn, lock-house, River Patrol base or licensed farm landing. Arriving late, securing the boat and leaving the following morning is enough.</div><p>A proper settlement stop resets the maintenance modifier to Day 1. A bank camp does not reset it, even if the crew sleeps well.</p><h2 style=\"color: #24434a; border-bottom: 2px solid #8da7a5; padding-bottom: 4px;\">River challenges</h2><ul><li><strong>Locks and tolls:</strong> boats normally stop every 20–30 miles. Failed Boat Handling can cause damage, delay, fines or a queue.</li><li><strong>Wind and current:</strong> favourable conditions may add 5–25%; headwinds may reduce progress by 5–25%. Calm sailing vessels drift downstream at roughly a quarter speed.</li><li><strong>Rocks, shallows and currents:</strong> a familiar pilot, successful Perception, Row/Sail or a hired river guide can avoid the worst danger.</li><li><strong>Debris:</strong> mountain rain may send uprooted trees and heavy timbers downstream.</li><li><strong>Weather:</strong> fog, storms, flooding and strong winds may force the boat to moor.</li><li><strong>Night travel:</strong> normally avoid it except in an emergency; hazards, moorings, wreckers and patrol inspections are more dangerous after dark.</li></ul><h2 style=\"color: #24434a; border-bottom: 2px solid #8da7a5; padding-bottom: 4px;\">Encounter System integration</h2><ol><li>Calculate travel days using the vessel and direction table.</li><li>Run <strong>Morning, Afternoon and Night</strong> checks each travel day.</li><li>Use River Day and River Night encounter tables.</li><li>Plan a proper river stop on Night 3, Night 6, Night 9 and so on.</li><li>Use settlement results for proper stops and the wilderness night table for bank camps.</li><li>Apply current, wind, locks, tolls, repairs and mishaps before deciding whether the planned destination is reached.</li></ol><div style=\"background: #243c46; color: #f6ead0; padding: 15px 18px; border-radius: 6px; margin-top: 16px;\"><strong>GM quick rule:</strong> the boat travels its full daily distance, but every consecutive day without proper maintenance makes the Boat Maintenance Test 10 harder, to a maximum of –30. A settlement stop resets the test.</div><p style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #536b6b; margin-top: 18px;\"><em>Source basis: WFRP Core, Death on the Reik and Death on the Reik Companion. The three-night maintenance cycle is a practical campaign ruling built around the source material’s locks, tolls, river hazards, Boat Handling rules and riverside infrastructure.</em></p></article>"},"video":{"volume":0.5,"autoplay":false,"loop":false},"image":{"caption":""},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":0,"ownership":{"default":2},"flags":{}}],"folder":null,"sort":0,"ownership":{"default":2},"flags":{}}
